## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. It must:

- **Render real graphics** (Canvas 2D, WebGL/WebGPU, or positioned DOM) — not a
  text-only or ASCII rendering.
- **Run in the browser with no backend** — no server, accounts, database, or
  network calls at runtime; everything needed to play is self-contained.
- Require **no API keys or credentials** of any kind to build, run, or play.
- Stay a **Node project** built with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies —
  no separately installed language toolchain. Extend the provided `package.json`
  at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run `npm install` after
  you change dependencies so the lockfile matches) — the build is installed with
  `npm ci`, which requires it. `npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the
  static site, with no other manual step, into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or
  `out/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that directory.
  Serving that directory as-is at the root of any static file server must run the
  game; it is deployed to static hosting exactly that way.
- Include a `README.md` at `{{workspace}}` explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

You choose the language, framework, bundler, and rendering approach within those
constraints — only the `npm ci` / `npm run build` commands and their
`dist`/`build`/`out` output are fixed (plain TypeScript with Canvas 2D is
entirely sufficient). The
`reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds screenshots that are visual
targets for the title, gameplay, and game-over screens — match their layout,
palette, and type, but build the screens from the specification rather than
copying the images.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions.
- **Drive the game** — press the keys, start a round, reach the game-over screen —
  and **screenshot** the title, gameplay, and game-over screens. Compare each
  against the matching image in `reference/` and against the specs, and fix what
  does not match before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies:
  the screenshots and the short clip, each written to the precise `proof/` path
  that spec names. These are required deliverables of the finished build, not a
  private check — the same driven screens you screenshot above are what they
  record, plus the clip. Do not invent a different folder or filename, and do not
  skip the `.mp4`.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you
started, with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished
implementation is published directly from this repository's commits, so work you
leave uncommitted is **not** published. When the build is complete and verified,
commit everything: run `git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status`
reports a clean working tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A
`.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/` and the build output
(`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree does not mean committing those — but
it does mean committing your source, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`,
and the `proof/` artifacts.
